ce503569-4c22-488b-b160-618df1210363	2026-05-28 18:51:14+00	git	fix: rip out worker, restore cluster-force-in-main-thread for cluster separation	The worker architecture was fighting three-forcegraph's lifecycle. Even with\nposition-cache injection, the library kept setting engineRunning=false then\nrelying on our reheat — meaning obj.position only got synced from node.x/y/z\nduring a brief window each frame. Combined with the d3.nodes(newArray) reset\non every graphData prop change, clusters never reliably separated even when\nthe worker correctly assigned them to different sectors.\n\nSwitch back to the pattern that worked pre-refactor: a single d3 simulation\nrunning inside the library itself, with a custom cluster spring force\nregistered via fg.d3Force('cluster', ...). The force pulls each node toward\nits component's sector center every tick. charge.distanceMax (190) is set\nwell below CLUSTER_D (600), so cross-cluster charge is identically zero —\ncomponents mathematically cannot merge.\n\nWhat's new vs. the original pre-refactor implementation:\n- graphComponents from Blue Team OVERRIDES BFS-based compId assignment, so\n  fraud graphs identified by the backend land in distinct sectors even when\n  the underlying transaction graph is topologically one component.\n- Sector seeding lives in mergedData (sync) AND in the force's initialize()\n  callback so fresh nodes never spend a frame at d3's near-origin default.\n- forceCenter is removed (the prior single global center is exactly what\n  collapsed clusters back to origin).\n\nRemoves the entire worker code path (graphWorld.worker.ts is no longer\nimported by GraphScene). Leaving the file in place for now — v2 cinematic\napp still uses it independently.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.7 <noreply@anthropic.com>	episodic	null	\N	0.8	[-0.08284005,-0.09049934,-0.021272924,0.016909314,0.07288293,-0.08817801,-0.10394934,-0.07769845,-0.03966095,0.013622084,-0.014269728,-0.00892057,-0.03983293,-0.013674932,0.0738953,-