d2d3abb5-c56f-4aab-8fbe-47dcef27531d	2026-05-28 12:11:52+00	git	fix: keep Three.js meshes live after simulation cools + restore camera zoom	Root cause: react-force-graph-3d sets engineRunning=false when d3 alpha\ndrops below alphaMin (~6 s after mount). After that, the library stops\ncalling node.__threeObj.position.set(x,y,z) each frame, so all mesh\npositions freeze at origin regardless of what we write to node.x/y/z.\nBlue Team analysis arrives well after 6 s, so worker positions were\nwritten to node.x but Three.js objects never moved.\n\nFixes:\n- onEngineStop: immediately call d3ReheatSimulation() to keep the\n  simulation alive forever. With all forces null, each reheated tick\n  just applies x=fx (our pinned positions) at near-zero CPU cost.\n- Tick handler: also write directly to n.__threeObj.position.set()\n  as belt-and-suspenders (works even during the brief engineRunning=false\n  window between 'end' event and reheat).\n- Restore initial zoomToFit(600ms delay) when first nodes arrive —\n  this was accidentally removed in the previous commit.\n- Cluster zoom now fires on ANY count change (not just increases),\n  catching the BFS→group-based sector transition edge case.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 4.6 <noreply@anthropic.com>	episodic	null	\N	0.6	[-0.05296851,-0.08843702,-0.035748173,-0.007112623,0.051084623,-0.054591496,-0.047598746,-0.03987233,0.020891756,0.040707752,-0.027207967,-0.024918916,-0.07142987,-0.018012945,0.056112643,-0.017820597,-0.003337141,-0.0096047465,-0.09038853,-0.009125497,-0.0040406245,-0.097569704,0.08360141,0.063639745,0.06618645,0.027670322,-0.015818642,-0.029278282,0.023589218,-0.0022361635,-0.023626491,-0.029459478,-0.07950143,-0.03985451,0.06174462,0.10422333,-0.08494995,-0.08283732,-0.1379238,0.061555557,0.025210759,0.14739962,0.0073962016,-0.027616635,0.05925576,0.07051643,-0.030507518,-0.015958628,0.050890066,-0.047268257,-0.027374234,-0.067116514,0.028228892,0.0227048,-0.00250033,0.079165146,0.046625335,-0.004891128,5.